End-Stage Liver Disease
Palliative & End of Life
Diuretic-resistant ascites, Hepatic encephalopathy/asterixis, Profound jaundice

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-027
Reason for Visit: Acute worsening of confusion, extreme lethargy, and massive abdominal distension causing respiratory compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, adult daughter
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient has become suddenly disoriented, extremely lethargic, and unable to converse, coupled with a massively distended, painful abdomen. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to evaluate for hepatic encephalopathy and spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, manage the severe mechanical dyspnea from ascites, and determine if an emergency hospital transfer was required to manage impending hepatic coma prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Daughter states: "He doesn't know where he is, his hands are flapping wildly, and his stomach is so huge he can barely take a breath."
History of Present Illness: Over the past month, the patient has developed massive, tense, and entirely diuretic-resistant ascites. Today, the patient developed hepatic encephalopathy clinically presenting with altered mental status, severe confusion, and distinct asterixis. The daughter reports profound cutaneous jaundice and severe scleral icterus worsening over the last week. There is a history of recurrent, severe episodes of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis. The patient exhibits fetor hepaticus presenting as a distinct, musty, sweet breath odor. The daughter notes caput medusae presenting as distinctly engorged superficial abdominal veins, along with spider angiomata distributed prominently over the upper torso, chest, and face. Palmar erythema and severe, generalized muscle wasting are starkly apparent.
Functional Status: Bedbound today due to lethargy and mechanical weight of the ascites; requires full assistance with all ADLs.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls due to encephalopathy and asterixis; high risk of catastrophic variceal hemorrhage.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/55 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 95% on room air | Wt: 78 Kg (predominantly ascitic fluid)
General: Profoundly jaundiced, cachectic male with a massive abdomen, lethargic but rousable, smelling strongly of fetor hepaticus.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals disorientation to time and place, sluggish mentation, and a striking, coarse flapping tremor (asterixis) upon wrist extension. Abdominal exam demonstrates massive, tense ascites with shifting dullness, positive fluid thrill, and prominent caput medusae. The abdomen is diffusely tender but lacks board-like rigidity. The diagnostic tetrad is fulfilled by the observed massive diuretic-resistant ascites, the classic hepatic encephalopathy/asterixis, and the profound cutaneous jaundice.
Environmental Observations: Lactulose bottles are present but mostly full, indicating poor recent compliance.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has missed several days of lactulose due to the severe nausea caused by the abdominal distension, directly precipitating the current encephalopathic crisis.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: End-Stage Liver Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Hepatic Encephalopathy and Tense Ascites)
Differentials:

Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP) - Cannot be completely ruled out due to the low-grade fever and diffuse abdominal tenderness; requires formal diagnostic paracentesis if goals of care support hospital transfer.

Subdural hematoma - Ruled out/unlikely as the cause of confusion, given the bilateral asterixis, classic fetor hepaticus, and known non-compliance with lactulose strongly pointing to a metabolic, hepatic etiology.

Acute variceal hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely at this exact moment due to the absence of hematemesis or melena, though the risk remains extremely high given the portal hypertension.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering an acute crisis of hepatic encephalopathy and mechanical respiratory compromise driven by end-stage liver disease and tense ascites. The objective findings of asterixis, jaundice, and altered mentation confirm the accumulation of neurotoxic ammonia secondary to missed lactulose doses. Because the patient is rapidly slipping into a hepatic coma and is suffering significant dyspnea from the massive ascites, delaying medical care until standard hours would result in irreversible neurological decline and profound suffering. Immediate after-hours assessment was critical to administer stat enteral treatments to clear the ammonia and arrange urgent ambulatory paracentesis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a stat dose of oral lactulose and prescribed a rigorous schedule to induce 3-4 soft bowel movements daily. Administered subcutaneous haloperidol for nausea to facilitate the oral lactulose. Discussed Advanced Care Directive; arranged urgent referral for ambulatory therapeutic paracentesis tomorrow morning for comfort.
Positive Behavioral Change: When the patient experiences nausea (trigger), explicitly use the prescribed anti-nausea wafer under the tongue 30 minutes before the lactulose dose (behavior). This ensures the lactulose stays down to clear the brain toxins, preventing the confusion and coma from returning (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community nursing to assist with medication compliance and monitor encephalopathy.
DME/Safety (Optional): Implement a bedside commode to manage the expected frequent bowel movements safely.
Follow-up: Ambulatory care unit review tomorrow for fluid drainage; GP review post-procedure.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient begins vomiting fresh blood or "coffee grounds," passes black, tarry stools, becomes completely unarousable, or develops a high spiking fever with severe, rigid abdominal pain, the daughter must call 000 immediately for emergency transfer.

